Debug.Assert(_items.Length == _size + emptySlots && emptySlots <= _size);
</code>

This assert statement is based on the comment "@@@ size of array does not change after the operation". It's checking if the length of the `_items` array is equal to the count of the items plus the number of empty slots, and the number of empty slots is less than or equal to the count of the items.
```cs
public void EnsureCapacity(int min)
{
    if (min > _items.Length) // the array is too small
    {
        // compute a new size
        int num = _items.Length * 2;
        if (num < min)
            num = min;
        else if (num > Int32.MaxValue)
            num = Int32.MaxValue;

        // create a new array
        Object[] newItems = new Object[num];

        // copy the items to the new array
        Array.Copy(_items, newItems, _size);
        _items = newItems;
    }
}
```
The `EnsureCapacity` method doubles the array length if the current capacity is less than the required minimum capacity. After doubling, it copies the old items to the new array.

The assert statement can be derived from multiple parts of the code:

* Initial array length (`new Object[num]`) vs final array length (`_size`).
* The array may not get smaller in the EnsureCapacity() method so `num < min` has to hold.
* The array length after doubling can't be larger than `Int32.MaxValue`.

Therefore, the assert statement is:
```cs
Debug.Assert(num >= _size && num <= min && num < Int32.MaxValue);
